 "segments": [
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "A guest has bitten on something hard in his dessert, and he's furious. He turns on Louis until Lyova steps in. Here's the argument in Russian.",
   "gap": 0.8
  },
  {
   "v": "guest",
   "t": "Что это у меня в десерте?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "guest",
   "t": "Я из-за вас чуть зуб не сломал!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "louis",
   "t": "Простите нас, пожалуйста.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "guest",
   "t": "Ты сначала по-русски говорить научись!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "guest",
   "t": "Зачем ты сюда приехал?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "guest",
   "t": "Чтобы люди ломали зубы?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "louis",
   "t": "Я кондитер, это моя вина.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "lyova",
   "t": "Оставьте кондитера в покое.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "guest",
   "t": "А ты кто такой?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "lyova",
   "t": "Я шеф-повар этого ресторана.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "lyova",
   "t": "Я отвечаю за всё на кухне.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "lyova",
   "t": "У вас есть претензии ко мне?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "guest",
   "t": "Вот моя претензия, держи.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "louis",
   "t": "Вкусный десерт, большое спасибо.",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Back through the argument, slowly.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "guest",
   "t": "Что это у меня в десерте?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'What is this in my dessert?' Now let's consider the dialogue: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "guest",
   "t": "у",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "guest",
   "t": "меня",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is how Russian says something is yours or happening to you.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "guest",
   "t": "Я из-за вас чуть зуб не сломал!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I almost broke a tooth because of you!' Now let's examine the words: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "guest",
   "t": "чуть",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is 'nearly', literally 'a hair not'. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "guest",
   "t": "из",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "-",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "guest",
   "t": "за",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "guest",
   "t": "вас",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is 'because of you', and here it takes in the whole restaurant.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "louis",
   "t": "Простите нас, пожалуйста.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Please forgive us.' We can focus on the dialogue: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "louis",
   "t": "Простите",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " gives the polite 'forgive', the one for strangers. Louis says 'us', ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "louis",
   "t": "нас",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", apologising for the kitchen.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "guest",
   "t": "Ты сначала по-русски говорить научись!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'First, learn to speak Russian!' We can analyze this part: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "guest",
   "t": "научись",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is the order form of 'learn', and using it with a stranger is the rude part. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "guest",
   "t": "по",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "-",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "guest",
   "t": "русски",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " comes before the verbs.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "guest",
   "t": "Зачем ты сюда приехал?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Why did you come here?' Now let's focus on the words: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "guest",
   "t": "приехал",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is 'came', by car or train, not on foot. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "guest",
   "t": "сюда",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is 'to here', the going partner of 'here'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "guest",
   "t": "Чтобы люди ломали зубы?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'So people would break their teeth?' After ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "guest",
   "t": "Чтобы",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", meaning 'so that', the verb goes into the past form: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "guest",
   "t": "ломали",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", plural for people. That's a fixed rule, even about the future.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "louis",
   "t": "Я кондитер, это моя вина.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I am the pastry chef, it is my fault.' Now let's analyze this part: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "louis",
   "t": "кондитер",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is a pastry chef. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "louis",
   "t": "вина",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " means blame, and it happens to look like the word for 'of wine', though that one has a different stress.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "lyova",
   "t": "Оставьте кондитера в покое.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Leave the pastry chef alone.' Let's examine the words: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "lyova",
   "t": "Оставьте",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " literally means 'leave', and the expression means 'leave in peace'. The pastry chef becomes ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "lyova",
   "t": "кондитера",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " because he's the person being left alone.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "guest",
   "t": "А ты кто такой?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'And who are you?' Now we can inspect the phrase: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "guest",
   "t": "ты",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "guest",
   "t": "кто",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "guest",
   "t": "такой",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is pointed and a bit rude. People say it to ask what right someone has, and using the informal 'you' to a stranger makes it ruder still.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "lyova",
   "t": "Я шеф-повар этого ресторана.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I am the head chef of this restaurant.' Let us inspect the words: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "lyova",
   "t": "этого",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "lyova",
   "t": "ресторана",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is 'of this restaurant', where the endings of both words mark the 'of'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "lyova",
   "t": "Я отвечаю за всё на кухне.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I am responsible for everything in the kitchen.' Lyova uses ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "lyova",
   "t": "отвечаю",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " to mean he answers for it all. And it's ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "lyova",
   "t": "на",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "lyova",
   "t": "кухне",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", 'on the kitchen', where English says 'in'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "lyova",
   "t": "У вас есть претензии ко мне?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Do you have complaints against me?' We will examine the dialogue: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "lyova",
   "t": "У",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "lyova",
   "t": "вас",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "lyova",
   "t": "есть",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is the way you say 'you have'. ",
